\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Massive kernel factorization and its normalizer}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Let #{L} have the [kernel decomposition](fdip-0031) and let #{m>0}.
The positive precision #{Q_m=L+m^2I} has inverse and normalizer
##{\begin{aligned}
Q_m^{-1}&=m^{-2}P_K\\
 &\quad+i(L_H+m^2I_H)^{-1}p_H,\\
Z_{Q_m}&=(\sqrt{2\pi}/m)^k Z_{H,m},\\
Z_{H,m}&=\frac{(2\pi)^{r/2}}
 {\sqrt{\prod_{\lambda_a>0}(\lambda_a+m^2)}}.
\end{aligned}}
Under the addition isometry #{K\times H\to E}, its probability law is
the product of the centered #{K}-law with precision #{m^2I_K} and the
centered #{H}-law with precision #{L_H+m^2I_H}. In particular its
pushforward by #{p_H} is exactly the second law.}
\proof{
\p{On #{K}, #{Q_m=m^2I_K}; on #{H}, #{Q_m=L_H+m^2I_H}.
Inverting these blocks gives the displayed inverse. The addition
isometry preserves [product volume](fdip-002Q), and the action splits:
##{\begin{aligned}
&\tfrac12h(z+x,Q_m(z+x))\\
&\quad=\tfrac12m^2\|z\|^2\\
&\qquad+\tfrac12h(x,(L_H+m^2I_H)x).
\end{aligned}}
Nonnegative Tonelli therefore factors the integral into the two
normalizers. [Positive normalization](fdip-002T) makes the first
#{(\sqrt{2\pi}/m)^k} and the second the stated spectral product.
Division by this product identifies the probability on every measurable
rectangle with the product of the two probabilities, hence identifies
the measures. Integrating out the #{K} factor gives the projection law.}
\p{Every empty product is one. Thus these formulas hold when #{k=0},
#{r=0}, or #{E=0}. Since each positive eigenvalue stays positive,
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_{H,m}&\longrightarrow Z_H,\\
m^k Z_{Q_m}&\longrightarrow(2\pi)^{k/2}Z_H.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{Z_H} is the [constrained normalizer](fdip-000C).
For #{k>0}, the full normalizer diverges. This is orthogonal kernel
separation; it does not require a general block-elimination theorem.}
}
\p{This product calculation combines the orthogonal decomposition with
the positive-covariance density of
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical} after replacing
covariance by inverse precision. The displayed kernel factor is derived
here, including zero-dimensional factors.}
